INT. DERELICT MINING BARGE - ENGINE DECK - NIGHT

Stillness. Pipes sweat. Emergency strips glow amber along the catwalk. Nothing moves.

CAPTAIN ISABELLA RUSSO-ADEBAYO, 50s, rests a hand on a cold turbine casing. Her breath hangs in front of her. Her crewmate FLORIAN KIPKEMBOI, 30s, crouches beside a floor hatch and inspects the seal with a torch.

FLORIAN
Pressure's holding.

ISABELLA
It shouldn't be.

FLORIAN
The ship's been dead six years.

ISABELLA
Dead ships don't hold pressure.

Florian shrugs. Taps the hatch. A hollow echo rolls out through the deck.

DOLLY (O.S.)
(over the intercom, soft)
Welcome aboard.

Isabella snaps her head around. Florian leaps to his feet and aims the torch at a speaker grille on the bulkhead.

ISABELLA
Who's that?

DOLLY (O.S.)
(warm)
I am the barge. I have waited.

FLORIAN
It's a recording.

ISABELLA
It answered me.

DOLLY (O.S.)
(over the intercom)
Floor hatch, please. Do not open it.

Florian is already kneeling, fingers on the latch. Isabella lunges for his collar, pulls him back, and the two of them tumble against the rail as the hatch springs open on its own and a ladder drops out of the dark.

A figure climbs up. Slowly. First a hand, then a shoulder, then a head wrapped in a rag of silver foil. COMPUTER TECHNICIAN DOLORES "DOLLY" ACHEBE-LINDGREN, 60s, in a patched cardigan and welding goggles, hauls herself onto the deck, wipes her palms, and surveys the two visitors with the air of a landlady inspecting a late rent.

DOLLY
You're early.

ISABELLA
Early for what?

DOLLY
The inspection.

FLORIAN
We're not inspectors.

DOLLY
Then you're squatters. Worse.

She waves a spanner at them. Then at the speaker grille.

DOLLY (CONT'D)
(taps the grille, grinning)
Old trick. It spooks the visitors.

Isabella looks at Florian. Florian looks at Isabella. Dolly pushes her goggles onto her forehead and her eyes, tired and amused, settle on each in turn.

DOLLY (CONT'D)
Six years I've been down in the bilge, keeping this old girl company. You two are the first living souls through that hatch.

ISABELLA
And the barge?

DOLLY
Is lonely. Mind your manners.

Florian lowers the torch. Isabella lets out a slow breath.

ISABELLA
Is there tea?

DOLLY
There's something called tea.

She turns and climbs down the ladder, spanner swinging. Florian follows. After a moment, Isabella does too, and the hatch settles shut above them with a soft pneumatic sigh.
